In many places, but in `make_watertight` in particular, the error codes (usually named `rval`) we receive from MOAB are checked with a pattern like: 

```cpp
if (rval != MB_SUCCESS) {
  // handle error here
  return rval;
}
```

or 

```cpp
assert(rval == moab::MB_SUCCESS);
```

MOAB provides a set of macros to set (or set and check) its internal error code and report error messages when appropriate,. The patterns above should be replaced with those macros (`MB_CHK_SET_ERR` or `MB_CHK_SET_CRR_CONT`) as appropriate to comply with best practices when writing code with the MOAB library.

